(* Ackermann's function with `%demand` (formerly accepted with `%mode`, by the demand-driven case of
   moded termination, Definition 10.3, which the redesign removes, REDESIGN D2/D3). The input `R1` of the
   second call is an answer of the first, so the demand rule `ack.check (M - 1) R1 :- ack.check M N, …,
   ack M (N - 1) R1` keeps that call: the demand relation and `ack` form one component. Its demand rules
   descend (A) and its answer rules are guarded by the demand (B), but a component mixing the two
   directions is not supported (docs/NOTES.md, C3, known limitations; issue #2). *)
ack : (m : int) -> (n : int) -> (r : int) -> rel.
%demand ack +m +n -r.
ack 0 N R :- R = N + 1. (*~ E0603 *)
ack M 0 R :- M > 0, ack (M - 1) 1 R.
ack M N R :- M > 0, N > 0, ack M (N - 1) R1, ack (M - 1) R1 R.
?- ack 2 3 R.
